---
import Resumen from "../../layouts/Resumen.astro";
import { OPT_IN_TTL_DAYS } from "../../lib/subscriber/policy.ts";

/**
 * The link does not work, and this page cannot say which kind of not working it is.
 *
 * Four cases land here and all four are the same sentence to the person reading it: the token
 * was never real, the seven days ran out, the daily purge already destroyed the row, or
 * **the link was already used**. That last one is the ticket's second acceptance criterion,
 * and the reason it arrives here rather than at a page of its own is structural: single use is
 * enforced by deleting the row, so a second visit finds exactly what an invented token finds,
 * and there is nothing left to tell the difference from.
 *
 * That is not a shortcut, it is the design working. A page saying "this link was already
 * used" would need the row to survive its own redemption, which is the state flag
 * `consumePendingOptIn()` exists not to have — and it would answer, for any token anyone
 * cares to try, whether it was ever a real one.
 *
 * The way back is the form and not a "resend" button. Re-sending would need the address, and
 * the only place it was held is the row that is gone.
 */
---

<Resumen title="El enlace ya no sirve" heading="Ese enlace ya no sirve">
  <p data-testid="dead-link">
    Puede que ya lo hayas usado — sirve una sola vez — o que se haya vencido: los enlaces
    duran {OPT_IN_TTL_DAYS} días y después borramos la solicitud.
  </p>

  <p>
    Si todavía quieres recibir el resumen, vuelve a llenar el formulario y te mandamos un
    enlace nuevo.
  </p>

  <p>
    <!--
      Worth saying, because somebody who already activated and then clicks the link again in
      an old mail would otherwise read this page as having lost their search.
    -->
    Si ya lo habías activado antes, tu búsqueda sigue guardada y el resumen te va a llegar
    igual.
  </p>

  <p><a href="/resumen">Ir al formulario</a></p>
</Resumen>
